  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Thursday) \nIssued at 1226 AM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nIn the wake of the exiting aformentioned upper low, a second upper\nlevel low pressure system will form over southern CA today, then \neject northeastward over the Four Corners and CO Sunday and \nSunday night. This slow-moving system will again draw extremely \nrich subtropical moisture northward over the forecast area \nproducing scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms Saturday\nand Saturday night with the greatest coverage over central and \neastern areas. A line of clearing will advance eastward over \nwestern parts of the forecast area Sunday, then through central \nareas as well Sunday night, while convection remains very active \nfarther east. With unseasonably high PWATs reaching around 0.80\" \nalong the central mountain chain, and as much as 1.15\" farther \neast, some storms over central and eastern areas Saturday, then \nover eastern areas Sunday, will be capable of producing locally \nheavy rain at rates over 1 inch per hour. Rain amounts Saturday \nthrough Sunday night are currently progged to reach around\naround 0.50-3.0\" along and just east of the central mountain\nchain, and from 1-4\" farther east, with lighter amounts farther\nwest. NBM 90th Percentile amounts for this period reach a \nstaggering 3-7\" across the eastern plains, with locally higher \namounts possible across the northeast plains.\n\nA few to 4\" of snow are also forecast on the highest peaks of the\nSangre de Cristo Mountains Saturday night through Sunday night, \nwhere 700 mb temperatures look to bottom out around +2 to +4 C. \nOtherwise, high temperatures across the forecast area will \ncontinue to trend cooler through the weekend, when readings should\nvary from a few to around 9 degrees below 30-year averages.\n\nA south and southeasterly return flow of low level moisture looks\nto linger over the eastern plains Monday and Monday night,\nenabling additional perturbations crossing aloft to produce  \nscattered showers and thunderstorms. PWATs are forecast to trend \ndownward, so the risk of additional flash flooding will be low. \nOtherwise, drier and warmer weather is forecast Monday and Tuesday\nas an active storm track north of NM draws dry southwest winds \nover the forecast area. A few isolated storms may return to east \ncentral and southeast areas on Tuesday afternoon.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms may increase in coverage again across\nthe forecast area Wednesday and Thursday, depending on how much\nprogress another upper level low pressure system can make\nadvancing into the western US. At this time confidence is low in\nanother widespread rain event over the forecast area, since model\ndifferences in track and timing of the weather system are so\ngreat.",
